3. Your Consciousness Is a Geometric Shape

How can we describe consciousness in the language of physics? RSVP theory proposes an elegant and tangible answer: consciousness is a geometric structure.

The framework models consciousness as a stable, three-dimensional "Consciousness Manifold" existing within the higher-dimensional plenum. This isn't just a metaphor. In what Flyxion calls 'Lab 20,' the theory projects the global state of the fields into a 3D 'consciousness phase space' where every possible mental state has a coordinate. A particular cognitive state—a thought, feeling, or perception—is simply a point on the surface of this manifold.

The process of thinking is a "trajectory"—a path traced across this geometric landscape, buffeted by currents of "semantic wind." Stable concepts, memories, and beliefs act as "semantic attractors"—deep basins or valleys in the manifold that the flow of thought naturally settles into. The implications are staggering: it suggests that the feeling of 'thinking' is literally the sensation of a physical pattern moving across a geometric landscape inside your head, governed by the same laws of motion as a planet or a river.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


4. Reality Is a "Lossy Hologram" and Your Brain Is the Projector

To understand the next step, we have to look at how RSVP treats observation itself. According to the theory, no observer ever experiences the full, high-dimensional reality of the plenum directly. Instead, our perception is a holographic projection.

Think of a three-dimensional object casting a two-dimensional shadow. Our brains perceive a lower-dimensional projection of the plenum, and this projection is inherently "lossy," meaning a vast amount of information is always discarded. This "lossy hologram" is projected onto the "Consciousness Manifold" we discussed earlier. The surface of that geometric shape is your perceived reality—a beautiful, coherent, but ultimately incomplete shadow of the true plenum.

The brain's job, then, is not to passively receive reality but to actively reconstruct a coherent world from this limited and biased projection. This reconstruction relies heavily on our priors—our existing beliefs and expectations. The theory even offers a physical definition of hallucination: it's what happens when an observer's priors become stronger than the incoming sensory data, causing their reconstruction of reality to be dominated by expectation rather than observation.


