5. Societies and Myths Follow the Same Laws as Stars

The RSVP framework scales all the way up to social structures and even narrative. Using a system called the "Spherepop" calculus, it models institutions like cities or economies as "macroscopic plenum organisms"—thermodynamic systems that, like a star or a living cell, maintain their internal order by managing entropy flows across their boundaries.

But perhaps the most mind-bending extension is its application to narrative. Article VI of the theory, titled "Bruno's Ark," claims that myth is the "highest-compression adjoint-time projection of plenum structure available to human cognition."

Translated, this means our most foundational stories are not just cultural artifacts but are hyper-efficient encodings of the universe's fundamental laws. The "adjoint-time" detail is critical: it suggests that myths are essentially stories that run the laws of the universe backwards in time to reveal their compressed, foundational logic. This final step completes the theory's grand unification, bridging the gap between the hard sciences and the humanities by suggesting they are both describing the same underlying reality, just through different projections.


